that these experiences the tears flowed  as pacific islands community leaders and  those affected by the dawn raids  gathered in auckland's town hall they  came to hear new zealand's prime  minister jacinda raderne  say the words they've been calling for  for decades  today i stand on behalf of the new  zealand government  to offer a formal and unreserved apology  to pacific communities for the  discriminatory implementation  of the immigration laws of the 1970s  that led to the events of the dawn raids  the immigration crackdown in the 1970s  targeted pacific islanders  who had been brought here because of  labour shortages the often violent dawn  raids which the government has now  apologized for  saw police searching homes businesses  and even churches  looking for overstays to deport the  response from the police  was overwhelming it was very reactionary  and it was very violent the sense of  injustice is still felt today  mainly because it was only pacific  islanders who were targeted  not the majority of overstayers who were  at the time  from the united states or europe a  formal government apology for past  injustices is rare in new zealand and  has to meet strict criteria  including the victims being definable as  a distinct group that continues to  suffer harm  connected to those historic events today  many of those affected  feel a sense of shame about what  happened and are reluctant to talk about  it  even to their families you would have  just felt like  you were trampled on in some way  emotionally spiritually  in many many ways and i think that has  been intergenerational  leaders in new zealand's polynesian  communities welcome the apology  and they hope it'll help people of the  pacific islands move on  be able to stand proud because  of who we are and achieve the very very  basic and simple goals for why we even  came to this country  that's all we want they also hope saying  sorry will help build trust between the  island communities and the government  and ensure future generations feel  secure in new zealand  even if the apology has taken nearly 50  years  wayne hey al jazeera auckland
 

